    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A Port Arthur hurricane damage claim is rarely a single claim. Port Arthur sits on Sabine Lake a few miles from the Gulf, and Jefferson County is one of the first-tier coastal counties served by the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA). That means one storm can leave a homeowner dealing with a homeowners carrier, a TWIA windstorm policy, and a flood insurer at the same time, each with different adjusters, deductibles, and deadlines. This page explains how hurricane claims usually work here and where a Port Arthur Public Adjuster may help.</p>

<h2>Why Hurricane Claims in Port Arthur Are Different</h2>
<p>Southeast Texas has a long record of tropical systems. Hurricane Rita made landfall near the Texas/Louisiana border in September 2005 and caused widespread wind damage across the area. Hurricane Ike in 2008 produced a 14–15 foot surge at Sabine Pass, the highest water level ever recorded there; NOAA notes the surge did not top the Port Arthur seawall, but wave action on top of it did, and at least 4,000 homes flooded county-wide (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=136895" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>). Hurricane Harvey in 2017 brought rainfall totals over 40 inches across much of the county and flooded more than 64,000 homes, according to the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2017Harvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service Lake Charles</a>. Hurricane Laura in 2020 made landfall near Cameron, Louisiana, but still sent damaging winds into eastern Jefferson and Orange counties.</p>
<p>Each of those storms damaged property in a different way. Rita and Laura were mostly wind events here. Harvey was mostly a rainfall flood. Ike combined wind and surge. The cause of each piece of damage decides which policy responds, so the first job in any hurricane claim is sorting damage by cause. </p>

<h2>Which Policy Covers Which Hurricane Damage?</h2>
<p>The table below is a general guide. Your actual policy language controls, and some owners carry wind coverage through their homeowners carrier instead of TWIA.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of damage</th><th>Policy that usually responds</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Roof covering torn off, shingles lifted, siding blown loose</td><td>TWIA or homeowners wind coverage</td><td>Named-storm or wind/hail deductible usually applies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rain entering through a wind-created opening</td><td>TWIA or homeowners wind coverage</td><td>Many policies cover interior water only if wind first created an opening.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Storm surge or rising water inside the home</td><td>NFIP or private flood policy</td><td>Standard homeowners and TWIA policies generally exclude flood.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fallen tree on the house</td><td>Usually wind coverage</td><td>Debris removal may have its own sublimit.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spoiled food or extra living costs</td><td>Homeowners or TWIA, depending on policy</td><td>Additional living expense terms vary widely.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lost business income after a closure</td><td>Commercial policy with business income coverage</td><td>Waiting periods and civil-authority terms matter.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>When the same room has both wind-driven rain damage and floodwater, the carriers may each point to the other. Clear photos, water lines, roof damage records, and a room-by-room estimate help separate the two. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage in Port Arthur</a> goes deeper into that problem, and our <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> page covers the flood side.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/storm-damaged-neighborhood-roofs.webp" alt="Rows of storm-damaged roofs with missing shingles after a Gulf Coast hurricane" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>After a hurricane, damage often follows the same pattern across a whole neighborhood, which can help show the storm as the cause.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Hurricane Deductibles Can Be Larger Than You Expect</h2>
<p>Many Texas policies carry a named-storm or hurricane deductible expressed as a percentage of the dwelling limit. On a home insured for $250,000, a 2% deductible is $5,000 and a 5% deductible is $12,500. If you have separate wind and flood policies, each deductible applies to its own claim. Check your declarations page for the exact numbers, then use our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> or read <a href="/hurricane-deductible-texas-explained/">how hurricane deductibles work in Texas</a>.</p>

