<h2>Documenting Hurricane Damage</h2>
<p>Insurers and adjusters are stretched thin after a large storm. Thousands of claims open within days, and field adjusters are often brought in from out of state. A well-organized file helps your claim move.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photos and video before cleanup.</strong> Capture the roof, each elevation of the house, every damaged room, and any visible water line on walls.</li>
<li><strong>Mitigation records.</strong> Keep receipts for tarps, board-up, fans, dehumidifiers, and water extraction. Southeast Texas humidity makes fast drying important.</li>
<li><strong>Damaged materials.</strong> Keep samples of shingles, flooring, and drywall when practical, and photograph anything you have to discard.</li>
<li><strong>Contents list.</strong> Record damaged belongings with descriptions, approximate age, and replacement cost.</li>
<li><strong>Every letter and call.</strong> Note claim numbers, adjuster names, dates, and what was said.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> is a printable version of these steps.</p>

<h2>Common Disputes After a Hurricane</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind vs. water.</strong> The wind carrier says interior damage came from flooding; the flood carrier says it came from a roof leak.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-existing damage.</strong> The insurer attributes a worn roof or old leak stains to age rather than the storm.</li>
<li><strong>Scope too narrow.</strong> Estimates that patch a few roof squares when the slope cannot be repaired, or that leave out drywall, insulation, and trim behind wet areas.</li>
<li><strong>Code items left out.</strong> Port Arthur enforces the 2021 International Residential and Building Codes, and TWIA eligibility requires TDI windstorm inspection for re-roofs and structural repairs. Whether upgrades are paid depends on ordinance or law coverage. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> Under replacement cost coverage, insurers usually pay actual cash value first and release depreciation after repairs, within a deadline in the policy.</li>
</ul>
<p>If your hurricane claim has already been paid low, an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can compare the carrier’s scope with the actual damage.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurance company. On a hurricane claim that can include reviewing each policy, separating wind, water, and flood damage, building a line-item estimate in Xactimate, documenting the property with photos and moisture readings, and handling communication with each adjuster. If you also have TWIA coverage, our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page explains that process. Port Arthur Public Adjuster is a service of [[company]], TDI license #[[license]]. We cannot guarantee any result, and fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Serving Port Arthur and the Golden Triangle</h2>
<p>Hurricanes do not stop at city limits. We work with property owners in Port Arthur, Jefferson County, and surrounding Southeast Texas communities, including <a href="/service-areas/sabine-pass/">Sabine Pass</a>, Groves, Nederland, Port Neches, Beaumont, and Orange. Note that Orange County is not in the TWIA catastrophe area, so coverage there often looks different from coverage in Jefferson County. If a hurricane is approaching, our <a href="/hurricane-tracker/">hurricane tracker</a> can help you follow it.</p>
